From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2_codec2: complete the work of frames the encoder drops

V4L2Encoder passes a CAPTURE buffer to the component only when it holds
data. It drops empty buffers silently and never looks at
V4L2_BUF_FLAG_ERROR. EncodeComponent reports work items strictly in
order and waits for a bitstream buffer for every work item that has an
input frame, so a single frame the device returns no bitstream for holds
up every later work item. The codec then takes no more input and
produces no more output, and no error is reported: CCodec has no
deadline on queued work. The Qualcomm iris driver returns such buffers
when the firmware drops a frame (DROP_FRAME and DATACORRUPT become an
empty buffer with V4L2_BUF_FLAG_ERROR) or returns an empty one.

Pass a buffer without data, or with V4L2_BUF_FLAG_ERROR, to the
component as a dropped frame, unless it is the empty last buffer that
ends a drain. The encoder runs without B-frames, so bitstream buffers
come back in input order: the component marks the oldest work item
still waiting for output with C2FrameData::FLAG_DROP_FRAME, as
DecodeComponent does for frames it does not output, and completes it
without a buffer. The client gets one output buffer fewer. The timestamp
cannot be used to find the work item, as iris returns these buffers with
a zero timestamp. CSD is still taken from the first buffer with data.
When the frame of the EOS work item was dropped, the end of the drain
keeps the drop mark, so that work item completes too.

The encoder is set up without frame skipping and is not expected to drop
frames at all. If it drops 30 in a row, one second at the usual 30 fps,
the component reports C2_CORRUPTED, so the client can restart the codec
or fall back instead of waiting for output forever. A few dropped frames
still give a usable stream.

